I fixed #1248: when a hook hits the 5-second timeout, its error message now includes the stderr it had printed before it was killed. Commit ba3b3e24.

Before: Hook 'X' timed out after 5 seconds
After: Hook 'X' timed out after 5 seconds. stderr so far:\n<what it printed>

What changed (src/hooks.rs, 3780 → 3791 lines, within the module-size limit):
• Since #1247, a hook's stderr is already captured while it runs, but the timeout branch threw it away. It now takes what has been captured so far, the same way the normal-exit path does, and appends it using the same head/tail size cap and truncation marker. That meant hooks_stderr_capture.rs needed no new method.
• It still doesn't wait for the reader thread or for the pipe to close, because a process the hook started in the background can keep the pipe open.
• A hook that printed nothing, or only whitespace, keeps the old message exactly.
• I rewrote the old "always was" comment to say what happens now and why.

Tests (three new ones in src/hooks_stderr_drain_tests.rs):
• Marker case: a hook writes HOOK-MARKER-1248 to stderr, then sleeps 30 seconds. Before the fix it failed by name with left: "Hook 'drain-probe' timed out after 5 seconds"; now the error includes the marker. Because the sleep runs as a separate child that keeps the pipe open, this test also proves the timeout returns in under 10 seconds rather than waiting for the pipe to close.
• Silent hook: a hook that prints nothing gets exactly the old message.
• Large output: a hook that prints 50 KB is cut down, with the truncation marker, and keeps both its first and last lines.

Checks: clippy with -D warnings, cargo fmt --check, the full cargo test and the module-size test all passed. I made the ARCHITECTURE.md edit after those runs, but it changes no code.

Docs: nothing in docs/src describes hook timeouts, so there was nothing to update there. I added a dated note to the hooks.rs entry in ARCHITECTURE.md and marked the old "output is dropped" claim as superseded rather than deleting it.
